So, for example, if someone's giving a speech, they may be dragging it out.

251
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:18,920
Right.

252
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:20,360
And you're like, wait.

253
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:22,300
So someone's giving a very long speech.

254
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:25,800
You're like, oh, my God, he's dragging the speech out so long.

255
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:26,100
Yeah.

256
00:12:26,800 --> 00:12:27,640
When will he ever stop?

257
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:28,280
Right.

258
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:31,440
Or you could say in an argument, I'm tired of arguing.

259
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:32,960
Let's not drag this out anymore.

260
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:33,940
Let's just stop.

261
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:35,420
OK.

262
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:40,900
So to drag something out is usually time related to make things longer than necessary.

263
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:42,440
Exactly.

264
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:43,440
All right.

265
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:52,860
And for our last phrase, when Jeff was talking about the relationship, he says, I know a relationship has been on the rocks for some time.

266
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:54,280
OK.

267
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:56,420
So the relationship was on the rocks.

268
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:57,880
OK.

269
00:12:57,880 --> 00:12:59,800
So this is an interesting phrase.

270
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:00,920
Think about rocks.

271
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:03,060
You know, you see rocks on the ground in the ocean.

272
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:10,040
Something that's on the rocks, in this case, means something that is not going well.

273
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:11,000
Something that has problems.

274
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:12,040
Not going smooth.

275
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:13,040
Yes, it's not smooth.

276
00:13:13,060 --> 00:13:16,820
So, when Jeff says our relationship has been on the rocks, he's saying we fight a lot.

277
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:17,560
We have problems.

278
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:18,580
It's not working.

279
00:13:19,180 --> 00:13:24,160
So, if I say, my company has been on the rocks recently, it means it's not doing very well.

280
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:25,800
Business is not going well.

281
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:27,200
OK.

282
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:28,120
So, you can use this for different things.

283
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:31,340
Now, obviously, you may have heard it before for drinks.

284
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:32,840
You say, scotch on the rocks.

285
00:13:33,140 --> 00:13:33,280
Right.

286
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:33,760
That's different.

287
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:34,580
Be careful.

288
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:37,960
In that sentence, Marco's talking about ice.

289
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:39,160
Right.

290
00:13:39,160 --> 00:13:40,160
Yeah.

291
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:42,100
So, that's different if you ask for a drink on the rocks.

292
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:48,410
But if you describe yourself or your company or relationship as being on the rocks, it means you're having a tough time.

293
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:49,530
It's a bad situation.

294
00:13:50,150 --> 00:13:50,330
Exactly.

295
00:13:50,890 --> 00:13:52,070
So, it's a very useful phrase.

296
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:56,480
I think that's very native, very colloquial if you say something like this.

297
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:57,480
Absolutely.

298
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:57,950
It's a great phrase to use.

299
00:13:58,480 --> 00:13:59,480
I encourage you.

300
00:13:59,480 --> 00:14:03,330
I mean, I hope that your situation, your work and your relationships are not on the rocks.

301
00:14:03,960 --> 00:14:10,610
But I do hope that when you're practicing speaking, use this phrase a lot because it's really, really colloquial like you say, Marco.

302
00:14:10,730 --> 00:14:10,850
Yeah.

303
00:14:10,910 --> 00:14:11,510
It's really good.

304
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:15,680
So, I think we've taken a look at a lot of phrases, a lot of vocab.

305
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:28,240
Why don't we go back, listen to the dialogue for one last time.

306
00:14:43,310 --> 00:14:43,770
What was that?

307
00:14:46,840 --> 00:15:00,120
Exactly.

308
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:07,240
Madam please.

309
00:15:07,250 --> 00:15:08,650
disco albums back.

310
00:15:09,050 --> 00:15:12,890
Fine, but I'm keeping the antique gramophone as my grandfather gave it to me.

311
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:18,260
I believe that was a wedding present to both of us Joanne and you hardly ever use it.

312
00:15:18,520 --> 00:15:21,880
He's my grandfather and he never really liked you anyway.

313
00:15:22,300 --> 00:15:22,540
Whatever!

314
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:27,860
Alright I'll concede this silly gramophone if you agree that I get the silver tea set.

315
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:31,760
How typical, when are you ever going to use a silver tea set?

316
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:34,920
Fine, I don't want to drag this out any more than necessary.

317
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:36,440
What's next?

318
00:15:37,430 --> 00:15:38,550
What about these old photographs?

319
00:15:39,630 --> 00:15:40,090
Which ones?

320
00:15:41,110 --> 00:15:41,530
Let me have a look.

321
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:45,740
Oh wow look at that, that brings back memories.

322
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:47,300
Remember that?

323
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:51,020
Our trip to Italy, I remember that day.

324
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:54,760
We were going to visit the Trevi Fountain and we got caught in the rain.

325
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:57,500
And you looked so adorable with your hair all wet.

326
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:02,880
I did take a picture of you standing there in that little alley smiling and laughing in the rain.

327
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:06,060
Oh we really did have fun back then didn't we?

328
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:09,440
Oh Joanne, are we making a big mistake?

329
00:16:10,430 --> 00:16:15,730
I know our relationship has been on the rocks for some time, but are you sure we can't reconcile and try again?

330
00:16:16,250 --> 00:16:17,250
I still love you.

331
00:16:17,650 --> 00:16:19,250
Oh Jeff, I love you too.

332
00:16:20,050 --> 00:16:23,170
I'm so glad we didn't have to decide who keeps the motorcycle.

333
00:16:23,810 --> 00:16:24,350
The motorcycle?

334
00:16:25,190 --> 00:16:25,610
But that's mine!

335
00:16:27,680 --> 00:16:33,590
Alright we're back.

336
00:16:33,930 --> 00:16:36,230
So a very interesting dialogue all about divorce.

337
00:16:36,570 --> 00:16:42,890
Now this is a very common situation nowadays in many countries such as the United States or Europe.

338
00:16:43,210 --> 00:16:47,010
Divorce rates are very high lately like in the past 10-20 years.

339
00:16:47,330 --> 00:16:51,720
Yeah they do say that since maybe the 70s or 80s divorce rates have been on the rise.

340
00:16:52,130 --> 00:16:55,490
I think the average now in America is about 50%.

341
00:16:55,490 --> 00:16:57,990
Yeah it's like 5 in 10 marriages and in divorce.

342
00:16:58,290 --> 00:16:58,610
That's right.

343
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:10,960
And then from what I understand or I read once, I think out of those 50% that get divorced, if they remarry again the probabilities of those that get remarried are again half.

344
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:13,850
So most likely people will get divorced at least twice.

345
00:17:14,290 --> 00:17:14,710
That's true.

346
00:17:15,170 --> 00:17:29,510
Although sometimes you meet people who got married when they were very very young and they realized that they grew up a little bit and their personality changed or their goals changed and they divorced that one time but then later in life when they marry again they know who they are.

347
00:17:29,850 --> 00:17:30,870
They get married again and it's better.

348
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:36,640
So I think the situations are all quite different but it is true that there are a lot of a lot of divorces in America.

349
00:17:37,030 --> 00:17:38,090
What about Ecuador though?

350
00:17:38,120 --> 00:17:39,680
I mean is the divorce rate similar?

351
00:17:40,210 --> 00:17:41,210
No not really.

352
00:17:41,420 --> 00:17:49,160
I think people are still a little bit more old-fashioned in that sense like it was before you know it was even a little bit taboo to be divorced.

353
00:17:49,160 --> 00:17:50,360
Yeah.

354
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:54,830
But I don't think people get divorced as often especially if people have been together for quite a while.

355
00:17:55,810 --> 00:18:02,610
That's one of the things that surprises me a lot is I've met people who have been married 20-30 years and then get divorced.

356
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:11,700
It's like what if it's like you've been through the toughest times you know you've pretty much beat the odds and why do you get divorced at such a late age?

357
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:17,360
Yeah it does seem counterintuitive but it's possible that they were unhappy for that time and they didn't want to affect their children's lives.

358
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:18,040
Right.

359
00:18:18,040 --> 00:18:23,780
So when their children go to college or leave they say okay now I can I can you know make a big decision.

360
00:18:23,780 --> 00:18:27,360
I know in countries for example such as Chile divorce is still illegal.

361
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:28,890
I think in the Philippines as well.

362
00:18:29,030 --> 00:18:29,290
Really?

363
00:18:29,530 --> 00:18:31,110
Yeah it's still illegal to get divorced.

364
00:18:31,450 --> 00:18:33,050
Is that because they're very religious countries?

365
00:18:33,970 --> 00:18:36,390
I know Chile is not really that religious.

366
00:18:36,860 --> 00:18:48,590
I don't know what it is exactly by law but a lot of people I think it may be legal now I can't really remember very well I think one of our Chilean listeners can help us out here but I know a lot of people used to annul their marriages because of this.

367
00:18:48,840 --> 00:18:49,240
I see.

368
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:53,660
So they would be like oh I put the wrong address so no this marriage is not it's not valid.

369
00:18:53,660 --> 00:18:55,540
Right or I spelled my name wrong.

370
00:18:55,540 --> 00:18:55,900
Yeah yeah.

371
00:18:56,210 --> 00:18:56,450
Interesting.

372
00:18:56,930 --> 00:19:04,110
Well I'd be curious to know as you said whether any of our Chilean friends know more about this or for the rest of you in your country is divorce common?

373
00:19:04,410 --> 00:19:04,810
Is it uncommon?

374
00:19:05,170 --> 00:19:08,190
Are there laws that prohibit or that allow divorce?

375
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:11,250
Let us know on our website EnglishPod.com. Alright we'll see you guys there.

376
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:19,863
Bye.
